narrative:
## Interfaces: Defining Shared Behavior

An **interface** (also called a trait in some languages) defines a set of functions that types can implement. It's a contract for behavior, not data.

### Defining an Interface

```xiom
pub interface Printable {
  fn format(self) -> Str;
}
```

Any type that implements `Printable` must provide a `format` function that returns a `Str`.

### Implementing an Interface

```xiom
pub type Point = {
  x: Float64;
  y: Float64;
}

impl Printable for Point {
  fn format(self) -> Str {
    return "Point(" + self.x + ", " + self.y + ")";
  }
}
```

### Using Interfaces as Constraints

```xiom
fn print_twice[T: Printable](item: &T) -> Unit {
  io.println(item.format());
  io.println(item.format());
}
```

`T: Printable` means "T must implement the Printable interface."

### Built-in Interfaces

XIOM provides these derive-able interfaces:

| Interface | Purpose |
|-----------|--------|
| `Clone` | Deep copy |
| `Eq` | Equality comparison (`==`) |
| `Ord` | Ordering (`<`, `>`) |
| `Display` | Format for display |
| `Hash` | Hash for maps/sets |

Use `derive` for automatic implementation:

```xiom
pub type User = {
  name: Str;
  id: Int;
} derive[Clone, Eq]
```

### Interfaces vs Inheritance

Unlike class-based inheritance (Java, C#), interfaces don't define data — only behavior. A type can implement many interfaces, and you can implement an interface for any type, including types you didn't define.

### Practical Example

```xiom
pub interface Describable {
  fn description(self) -> Str;
}

pub type Book = { title: Str; pages: Int; }

impl Describable for Book {
  fn description(self) -> Str {
    return self.title + " (" + self.pages + " pages)";
  }
}
```

